All night long and
into the morning,

1005
00:58:23,367 --> 00:58:25,433
it's just been a
constant drizzle.

1006
00:58:25,600 --> 00:58:27,066
And it just sucks.

1007
00:58:27,233 --> 00:58:28,834
It's just abysmal,

1008
00:58:29,000 --> 00:58:30,934
really soggy out there.

1009
00:58:31,100 --> 00:58:32,766
I'm getting cold.

1010
00:58:32,767 --> 00:58:34,734
And I put some
more wood on the fire.

1011
00:58:34,900 --> 00:58:37,767
I'm looking at the amount
of wood I'm already burning.

1012
00:58:37,934 --> 00:58:40,266
I started grabbing wet
wood as soon as I got out

1013
00:58:40,433 --> 00:58:43,166
to put it in my shelter
to start drying it.

1014
00:58:43,333 --> 00:58:46,033
Everything around here is wet,
and that's my biggest concern

1015
00:58:46,200 --> 00:58:48,165
right now is that I've
only got wet wood.

1016
00:58:48,166 --> 00:58:51,100
If there's another night of
rain, it's gonna be harder.

1017
00:58:51,266 --> 00:58:53,233
I've got to keep my
fire lit so I don't sleep

1018
00:58:53,400 --> 00:58:54,934
in hypothermia out here.

1019
00:58:55,100 --> 00:58:56,767
This is the dry wood
that I had already.

1020
00:58:56,934 --> 00:58:59,366
It's very limited,
but I'm drying out

1021
00:58:59,367 --> 00:59:00,666
some of this wet
wood that I gathered

1022
00:59:00,667 --> 00:59:02,265
and I'm sticking
these small sticks

1023
00:59:02,266 --> 00:59:04,266
underneath the
embers to dry them out

1024
00:59:04,433 --> 00:59:06,166
and then they won't burn.

1025
00:59:06,333 --> 00:59:09,165
That's my goal here is
to have a store of wood

1026
00:59:09,166 --> 00:59:10,567
ready to go for tonight

1027
00:59:10,734 --> 00:59:12,266
so that I can just stay warm.

1028
00:59:12,433 --> 00:59:15,967
I bent up a piece to cover
the rain, and then I bent it

1029
00:59:16,133 --> 00:59:19,866
at an angle so that the water
runs down off of this bend

1030
00:59:19,867 --> 00:59:21,767
and away from the fire pit.

1031
00:59:21,934 --> 00:59:24,033
So while I could tighten
up the bricks a little bit,

1032
00:59:24,200 --> 00:59:25,634
keep some of the wind out of it,

1033
00:59:25,800 --> 00:59:28,233
put forward just a little bit.

1034
00:59:28,400 --> 00:59:31,867
But I give away a lot of
heat coming out of this thing.

1035
00:59:32,033 --> 00:59:33,133
Not as much as I'd like.

1036
00:59:33,300 --> 00:59:34,667
I mean, I might wanna put...

1037
00:59:35,567 --> 00:59:37,233
another heat reflector in there.

1038
00:59:38,667 --> 00:59:41,766
There's a ton of shot
up coffee cans out there.

1039
00:59:41,767 --> 00:59:43,333
I flattened them out
and smashed them

1040
00:59:43,500 --> 00:59:45,433
and lined the perimeter,
inside perimeter,

1041
00:59:45,600 --> 00:59:47,166
of the fireplace.

1042
00:59:47,333 --> 00:59:49,433
It might give me
a better heat wall.

1043
00:59:49,600 --> 00:59:52,000
And it's not raining too hard.

1044
00:59:52,934 --> 00:59:55,567
All right, so we're just
gonna try and smash them up,

1045
00:59:55,734 --> 00:59:57,834
make them, try
to make them flat.

1046
01:00:01,667 --> 01:00:02,967
I'm grateful for the table

1047
01:00:03,133 --> 01:00:05,467
that I found at
the hunter's lodge.

1048
01:00:05,634 --> 01:00:07,466
I thought about
making these a chimney,

1049
01:00:07,467 --> 01:00:08,834
but they're so full of holes,

1050
01:00:09,000 --> 01:00:10,566
you know, it really wouldn't
have been conducive.

1051
01:00:10,567 --> 01:00:12,734
But when I flatten
them, they kind of layer

1052
01:00:12,900 --> 01:00:14,467
over each other and
cover up the holes.

1053
01:00:16,634 --> 01:00:18,165
It's probably a more
elegant way of doing that,

1054
01:00:18,166 --> 01:00:19,767
but that's not
how we do it here.

1055
01:00:21,233 --> 01:00:24,367
This place is unrelenting
and unforgiving.

1056
01:00:24,533 --> 01:00:27,634
And I would not have
gotten past day five

1057
01:00:27,800 --> 01:00:28,867
losing my partners

1058
01:00:29,033 --> 01:00:30,566
if I didn't have these
man-made materials

1059
01:00:30,567 --> 01:00:32,934
to secure a shelter for myself.

1060
01:00:37,066 --> 01:00:40,265
Push the bricks
a little bit closer.

1061
01:00:40,266 --> 01:00:42,466
Ugh...

1062
01:00:42,467 --> 01:00:44,834
Make a little heat wall.

1063
01:00:45,000 --> 01:00:47,433
My first challenge in
Zambia, we used stone.

1064
01:00:47,600 --> 01:00:50,634
We had nice, big, flat
pieces that could retain heat.

1065
01:00:50,800 --> 01:00:54,667
But metal, you get all that
same stuff with less mass.

1066
01:00:56,667 --> 01:00:59,634
And I kind of placed them
around the back edge,

1067
01:00:59,800 --> 01:01:03,000
and over the top of the
fire pit so that it would

1068
01:01:03,166 --> 01:01:04,867
kinda collect and
bounce more heat,

1069
01:01:05,033 --> 01:01:06,366
like a little heat wall.

1070
01:01:06,367 --> 01:01:08,433
You know, my imagination, uh,

1071
01:01:08,600 --> 01:01:11,000
runs wild sometimes,

1072
01:01:11,166 --> 01:01:12,166
but when it's harnessed

1073
01:01:12,200 --> 01:01:13,634
and in the right
direction, and...

1074
01:01:14,834 --> 01:01:16,133
it can create amazing things,

1075
01:01:16,300 --> 01:01:18,566
and this is a real
testament to that.

1076
01:01:18,567 --> 01:01:21,934
Despite all this cold air
and the wind and rain,

1077
01:01:22,100 --> 01:01:23,634
you can already feel the heat.

1078
01:01:30,333 --> 01:01:33,367
Ooh! It's still raining.

1079
01:01:33,533 --> 01:01:35,367
I don't know what we're
gonna do about this.

1080
01:01:35,533 --> 01:01:37,934
Some of those racks
with the thin slices

1081
01:01:38,100 --> 01:01:40,866
are probably done, but
are they too hard to get to?

1082
01:01:40,867 --> 01:01:42,634
Should we just bring
the whole barrel in?

1083
01:01:42,800 --> 01:01:44,000
Probably unrealistic.

1084
01:01:44,166 --> 01:01:45,867
Barrel fitting through this?

1085
01:01:46,033 --> 01:01:47,199
Probably not.

1086
01:01:47,200 --> 01:01:49,667
It's been raining pretty much
since we had the kudu on.

1087
01:01:49,834 --> 01:01:51,934
I've thought about
bringing it inside the shelter,

1088
01:01:52,100 --> 01:01:53,500
but don't know if
that's gonna work.

1089
01:01:55,767 --> 01:01:57,509
I wanna take this down
and deal with the meat,

1090
01:01:57,533 --> 01:02:00,065
but it's just we can't
really take that off

1091
01:02:00,066 --> 01:02:03,133
and expose all that meat
to moisture and water.

1092
01:02:03,300 --> 01:02:05,265
It's raining and
that's gonna ruin it.

1093
01:02:05,266 --> 01:02:08,567
So we've probably got 40
pounds of meat in here right now.

1094
01:02:08,734 --> 01:02:10,934
And just with the
way this rain's going,

1095
01:02:11,100 --> 01:02:13,867
we're at risk of losing
a lot, if not most of it.

1096
01:02:14,867 --> 01:02:16,734
We have to keep the
temperature perfect.

1097
01:02:16,900 --> 01:02:18,634
We have to make sure
there's enough smoke.

1098
01:02:18,800 --> 01:02:20,467
We have to make
sure everything's right.

1099
01:02:20,634 --> 01:02:22,834
Now, add to that
the rain coming in,

1100
01:02:23,000 --> 01:02:24,265
the cool air coming in,

1101
01:02:24,266 --> 01:02:25,767
it makes it extremely difficult.

1102
01:02:25,934 --> 01:02:28,433
Nature's given us this gift,
and now she's making us

1103
01:02:28,600 --> 01:02:30,233
work harder to not lose it.

1104
01:02:31,533 --> 01:02:33,233
I do not like this at all.

1105
01:02:45,367 --> 01:02:48,100
Still going.

1106
01:02:48,266 --> 01:02:51,033
Yeah, it's having
a real crack today.

1107
01:02:51,200 --> 01:02:52,566
Haven't had any sleep.

1108
01:02:52,567 --> 01:02:54,634
We thought, with the
vehicles, we were a little bit

1109
01:02:54,800 --> 01:02:56,567
more invincible than
we actually were.

1110
01:02:56,734 --> 01:02:58,934
Oh no, that's very bad.

1111
01:02:59,100 --> 01:03:00,533
It's not working.

1112
01:03:00,700 --> 01:03:02,100
They leak like a sieve.

1113
01:03:02,266 --> 01:03:04,266
The drips were on the high side.

1114
01:03:06,133 --> 01:03:08,766
Let me start plugging holes.

1115
01:03:08,767 --> 01:03:10,767
You all right?

1116
01:03:10,934 --> 01:03:15,166
Yeah, I'm trying
to create a... there!

1117
01:03:16,166 --> 01:03:18,466
Now it shouldn't
drip over there.

1118
01:03:18,467 --> 01:03:20,766
Oh! You're such a
clever little cookie.

1119
01:03:20,767 --> 01:03:23,834
So I put the stick there,
which moved the drip.

1120
01:03:24,000 --> 01:03:25,360
Stick on there?
That's a good move.

1121
01:03:25,400 --> 01:03:26,400
No.

1122
01:03:26,433 --> 01:03:27,666
Maybe not.

1123
01:03:27,667 --> 01:03:30,066
It's just decided it's
gonna find a new way.

1124
01:03:30,233 --> 01:03:32,433
Yeah.

1125
01:03:32,600 --> 01:03:36,133
This is suffering at its utmost.

1126
01:03:36,300 --> 01:03:38,140
We're hunkered down.
There's nowhere we can go.

1127
01:03:38,300 --> 01:03:40,767
If we get out in the rain,
we'll get hypothermia.

1128
01:03:40,934 --> 01:03:42,032
So we need to stay here.

1129
01:03:42,033 --> 01:03:45,466
We need to be as dry as
we can because if we aren't,

1130
01:03:45,467 --> 01:03:47,934
it can take us out
of this challenge.

1131
01:03:48,100 --> 01:03:50,366
This is how I get
through the rain.

1132
01:03:50,367 --> 01:03:52,467
Arms crossed, legs together,

1133
01:03:53,767 --> 01:03:55,366
arms over my heart.

1134
01:03:55,367 --> 01:03:57,133
This is tapping weather,

1135
01:03:57,300 --> 01:03:59,634
if you don't have
anything, right?

1136
01:03:59,800 --> 01:04:00,800
Can be.

1137
01:04:01,867 --> 01:04:04,467
It's all... all depends
on the person.

1138
01:04:04,634 --> 01:04:07,666
You can't even have
that thought in your head.

1139
01:04:07,667 --> 01:04:08,734
No.

1140
01:04:08,900 --> 01:04:12,367
Once you even conceive
that as a possibility,

1141
01:04:12,533 --> 01:04:13,734
you're already out.

1142
01:04:14,767 --> 01:04:16,766
I think the hardest
part of this is

1143
01:04:16,767 --> 01:04:19,367
we have no control over
what the weather's doing.

1144
01:04:19,533 --> 01:04:20,967
This could be a day thing.

1145
01:04:21,133 --> 01:04:22,265
It could be a week thing.

1146
01:04:22,266 --> 01:04:24,967
It could be the rest
of the challenge thing.

1147
01:04:25,133 --> 01:04:28,266
This is a mental
breaker right here.

1148
01:04:28,433 --> 01:04:31,467
You know, as someone who's
done so many of these challenges,

1149
01:04:31,634 --> 01:04:33,667
you tend to forget how this is

1150
01:04:33,834 --> 01:04:36,366
their first
experience in Africa.

1151
01:04:36,367 --> 01:04:38,033
And it's terrifying.

1152
01:04:39,333 --> 01:04:41,367
Storms always finish,

1153
01:04:42,266 --> 01:04:44,166
just a matter of when.

1154
01:04:44,333 --> 01:04:46,667
The sun will come out again.

1155
01:04:46,834 --> 01:04:48,366
You can do
anything in the world,

1156
01:04:48,367 --> 01:04:50,634
no matter what
situation you're in.

1157
01:04:50,800 --> 01:04:52,467
A struggle is what makes a hero.

1158
01:05:11,433 --> 01:05:13,233
Oh,

1159
01:05:13,400 --> 01:05:14,967
The boma's...

1160
01:05:15,133 --> 01:05:17,433
-definitely flooding.
-Yeah.

1161
01:05:20,166 --> 01:05:22,766
It's kind of getting
real close to our fire.

1162
01:05:22,767 --> 01:05:24,133
All right.

1163
01:05:24,300 --> 01:05:25,266
Holy!

1164
01:05:25,267 --> 01:05:27,466
The whole place
is under water now.

1165
01:05:27,467 --> 01:05:29,366
Yeah.

1166
01:05:29,367 --> 01:05:30,967
Our boma is kind of in a threat

1167
01:05:31,133 --> 01:05:32,166
of being flooded.

1168
01:05:32,333 --> 01:05:34,734
If our boma gets
flooded, our fire goes out.

1169
01:05:34,900 --> 01:05:36,433
Hypothermia is a real thing.

1170
01:05:37,867 --> 01:05:39,100
Our fire is literally life.

1171
01:05:39,266 --> 01:05:41,100
If we lose that,
we're out of this.

1172
01:06:06,567 --> 01:06:08,566
I'm happy to jump on
rotation if that helps.

1173
01:06:08,567 --> 01:06:10,767
No, I got it. You
guys stay warm.

1174
01:06:12,734 --> 01:06:14,100
It's what I'm here for.

1175
01:06:14,266 --> 01:06:15,467
Thank you.

1176
01:06:15,634 --> 01:06:17,100
Don't even worry about it.

1177
01:06:17,266 --> 01:06:19,667
I really... I really
don't mind one bit.

1178
01:06:20,934 --> 01:06:22,934
It's "Naked and Afraid," baby.

1179
01:06:23,100 --> 01:06:25,266
If this wasn't a part of it,
I wouldn't even be here.

1180
01:06:34,767 --> 01:06:37,367
"Impossible" is just a word
somebody made up one time,

1181
01:06:37,533 --> 01:06:39,567
because they decided
something was too hard to do,

1182
01:06:39,734 --> 01:06:41,667
and I don't believe
in that one bit.

1183
01:06:41,834 --> 01:06:43,867
I love picking up
myself by my bootstraps

1184
01:06:44,033 --> 01:06:45,200
and getting the job done

1185
01:06:45,367 --> 01:06:47,200
to not only keep me
in this competition,

1186
01:06:47,367 --> 01:06:49,467
but my team,
keep our fire going.

1187
01:06:49,634 --> 01:06:51,333
Rain always stops.

1188
01:06:51,500 --> 01:06:53,380
It might not be today, it
might not be tomorrow,

1189
01:06:53,467 --> 01:06:55,066
but eventually the storm ends.

1190
01:06:55,233 --> 01:06:57,233
And it's just being proactive

1191
01:06:57,400 --> 01:06:59,734
during the storm
while it's happening

1192
01:06:59,900 --> 01:07:01,367
to get through it.

1193
01:07:01,533 --> 01:07:02,566
You're a legend.

1194
01:07:02,567 --> 01:07:03,767
Thank you so much.

1195
01:07:05,667 --> 01:07:06,899
I'm in awe.

1196
01:07:06,900 --> 01:07:10,266
Steven is literally bailing us
out with a dead man's helmet.

1197
01:07:10,433 --> 01:07:12,266
He really has earned
his legend status.

1198
01:07:21,333 --> 01:07:24,166
Stop the flood. Our fire's safe.

1199
01:07:24,333 --> 01:07:27,400
We were about six
inches underwater.

1200
01:07:27,567 --> 01:07:30,400
And now, in the deepest
places, we're at like two inches.

1201
01:07:30,567 --> 01:07:32,367
So another beautiful
day in Africa.

1202
01:07:33,433 --> 01:07:36,166
Plus, from my experience,

1203
01:07:37,400 --> 01:07:38,767
it could always be worse.

1204
01:07:40,433 --> 01:07:42,400
Wee! It went.

1205
01:07:54,867 --> 01:07:57,200
This challenge, if it's the
sun, it's the hottest sun.

1206
01:07:57,367 --> 01:07:59,767
And if it's rain,
it's a monsoon.

1207
01:07:59,934 --> 01:08:02,567
I've gotta shore up my
shelter to keep the water out,

1208
01:08:02,734 --> 01:08:05,166
and I'll be a lot happier.

1209
01:08:05,333 --> 01:08:06,813
The wind's really
starting to pick up,

1210
01:08:06,900 --> 01:08:09,100
but this thing's holding strong.

1211
01:08:16,066 --> 01:08:18,200
I knew that was gonna happen.

1212
01:08:18,367 --> 01:08:21,166
The materials are brought
up like this, and there's a gap

1213
01:08:21,333 --> 01:08:24,000
in between where they don't
touch and overlap each other,

1214
01:08:24,166 --> 01:08:27,100
and so the ridge cap
will cover that gap.

1215
01:08:27,266 --> 01:08:28,666
I've gotta wrap
that thing on there.

1216
01:08:28,734 --> 01:08:29,866
It's gonna be difficult, though,

1217
01:08:29,867 --> 01:08:31,567
'cause it's at
its highest point,

1218
01:08:31,734 --> 01:08:33,000
higher than I am tall.

1219
01:08:33,166 --> 01:08:35,400
But right now, I'm
getting water in my shelter.

1220
01:08:35,567 --> 01:08:37,100
So I'm glad it happened now,

1221
01:08:37,266 --> 01:08:38,506
not in the middle of the night.

1222
01:08:39,634 --> 01:08:41,533
Ugh...

1223
01:08:44,033 --> 01:08:45,400
Working with this wire,

1224
01:08:45,567 --> 01:08:47,634
I don't have a pair of
pliers, anything to snip it,

1225
01:08:47,800 --> 01:08:50,867
so I just got to use friction

1226
01:08:51,033 --> 01:08:53,400
to bend it back and forth

1227
01:08:53,567 --> 01:08:55,734
until the metal
weakens and snaps.

1228
01:08:55,900 --> 01:08:58,300
If there was one item I
wish I had on this challenge,

1229
01:08:58,467 --> 01:08:59,834
it'd be a pair of pliers.

1230
01:09:01,133 --> 01:09:03,100
Nice.

1231
01:09:08,066 --> 01:09:10,734
This shelter was built
by materials that were just

1232
01:09:10,900 --> 01:09:12,266
by that destroyed
lodge over there

1233
01:09:12,433 --> 01:09:13,867
and dragged them over here

1234
01:09:14,033 --> 01:09:16,834
and assembled them pretty
quickly and got something.

1235
01:09:17,000 --> 01:09:19,967
And now... now I got the next,
you know, rest of my challenge

1236
01:09:20,133 --> 01:09:23,433
to really fine-tune it,
really make it, improve it.

1237
01:09:23,600 --> 01:09:25,066
That's really all I can do here,

1238
01:09:25,233 --> 01:09:26,867
is just keep making
it better and better.

1239
01:09:27,033 --> 01:09:30,266
So during the heat, it works,
and during the rain, it works.

1240
01:09:30,433 --> 01:09:32,333
And, you know, I
don't know about snow,

1241
01:09:32,500 --> 01:09:34,000
but it's very unlikely.

1242
01:09:34,166 --> 01:09:37,333
So, if that happens,
you know, well,

1243
01:09:37,500 --> 01:09:38,767
I think we're all in trouble.

1244
01:09:48,133 --> 01:09:50,867
What an absolute crappy day.

1245
01:09:51,867 --> 01:09:53,834
I mean, what a crappy day,

1246
01:09:55,834 --> 01:09:57,066
Yeah.

1247
01:09:58,600 --> 01:10:00,433
Not for me, buddy.

1248
01:10:01,333 --> 01:10:03,967
I can't believe the
consistency of the rain.

1249
01:10:04,133 --> 01:10:05,133
This heavy.

1250
01:10:06,066 --> 01:10:07,500
That long.

